preConditionHook:
// A BUY LIMIT A FIXED DISTANCE BELOW A STEEPLY RISING MOVING AVERAGE: the
// EMA is rising at least minMaSlopePerBar price points per bar over the
// last maSlopeLookback bars, and this bar trades down to intervalDistance
// points below it. Short is the mirror above a falling average.
//
// THE LIMIT ORDER under the corpus's standing signal-bar-close /
// next-bar-open convention: an order resting at a level is taken as filled
// on the bar that trades to the level, which is why the test is low[0]
// against a level read from the SAME bar's EMA. The merged pseudocode
// paired low[1] with the current bar's average -- yesterday's low against
// today's level -- a lag with no stated purpose; TrendLineTouch made the
// same substitution for R28-38's limit order at the line.
//
// THE LADDER IS DROPPED. The source places numScaleInLevels buy limits at
// successive intervals below the average, a multi-unit scale-in the engine
// cannot hold as one position; this is that ladder at one level, and
// intervalDistance is the offset of the sole order.
//
// EQUALS TWO PANES, recorded rather than hidden: EmaSlope ANDed with the
// limit touch, up to the unit change -- EmaSlope's threshold is a TOTAL
// move in ATRs, this one a PER-BAR slope in price points, which is the
// source's own statement. Carried whole on the EecBooksCanBeGreat
// precedent: a single named order placement stated by one source.
//
// A PRODUCT, NEVER A QUOTIENT. The source divides the EMA's move by
// maSlopeLookback; EasyLanguage raises a runtime divide-by-zero, so the
// per-bar slope floor is multiplied back out instead.
//
// intervalDistance is in PRICE POINTS, not ticks: MinMove/PriceScale are
// unregistered and the engine exposes no tick size to a strategy.
//
// emaLength is not in the merged parameter list at all -- the source says
// only 'the moving average' -- so it is added here at the corpus default
// of 20, the length every other EMA rule carries.
//
// MultiWalk's WFSafe_Xaverage, the function the EasyLanguage twin calls,
// ported unchanged from EmaGapBar (the EMA reference, which carries the full
// measurements). MEASURED, EL_XAverage_Probe.txt (@OJ 240min, chart starts
// 2007 and 2010):
//   * the wrapper is BIT-IDENTICAL to the plain XAverage built-in at a fixed
//     length -- (wf - xa) * 1e12 printed exactly 0.0000 on all 17,785 bars;
//   * the SEED is the PRICE on the study's first calculated bar
//     (ctx.CurrentBar() == 1), not a length-term simple average;
//   * the recurrence is x + sf*(p - x), ONE statement, never sf*p + (1-sf)*x:
//     the two separated on 67% of probed bars past 100. Do not re-associate;
//   * there is NO warm-up hole, so no bar-count guard returning 0 on the
//     CURRENT value;
//   * an EMA NEVER FORGETS ITS SEED -- it only decays at (1 - sf)^n, ~330
//     bars -- so Max Bars Back and the chart start are part of this rule's
//     specification.
// LENGTH CHANGE MEASURED 2026-09-17 (EL_XAverage_Lag_Probe.txt): the wrapper
// recomputes sf on the bar the length changes and carries its state, with no
// re-seed (reconstruction error 3.6e-15; the three rival hypotheses were off
// by 1.9 to 42 price units). emaLength MAY therefore be optimized in grids --
// the "pin emaLength" caveat is retired, and this mirror, which derives sf
// from the raw input every bar and carries emaVal, already behaves that way.
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the
// generator only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a
// redefinition when this rule is placed twice in one strategy.
emaSf = 2.0 / (emaLength + 1.0);
if (ctx.CurrentBar() == 1) {
    emaVal = close[0];
} else {
    emaVal = emaVal + emaSf * (close[0] - emaVal);
}
// LAGGED EMA READS, MEASURED 2026-09-17 (EL_XAverage_Lag_Probe.txt): the
// function's own [k] history is bit for bit the value it printed k bars
// earlier -- the lag1 and lag5 columns were exactly 0 on every bar past the
// lag on both charts (9,664 + 8,157 and 9,659 + 8,152 bars) -- and BEFORE
// that history exists ([k] on bars 1..k) the read returns 0.0, not the seed
// and not a -1 sentinel. So the zero-initialized mirrored ring buffer below
// is EXACT, and the rule GUARDS its own first k bars on the bar counter
// instead of comparing against that zero.
// Re-seeded if the depth ever changes, the way BarRangeAboveStd's
// mirrored history is.
emaDepth = (int)maSlopeLookback + 1;
if (emaDepth < 1) {
    emaDepth = 1;
}
if (emaHistory.size() != (std::size_t)emaDepth) {
    emaHistory.assign((std::size_t)emaDepth, 0.0);
    emaCursor = 0;
}
emaHistory[emaCursor] = emaVal;
emaLag = emaHistory[(emaCursor + emaHistory.size() - (std::size_t)((int)maSlopeLookback)) % emaHistory.size()];
emaCursor = (emaCursor + 1) % emaHistory.size();
slopeFloor = minMaSlopePerBar * maSlopeLookback;
buyLimitPrice = emaVal - intervalDistance;
sellLimitPrice = emaVal + intervalDistance;
// Deepest lagged read: ema[maSlopeLookback].
limitReady = ctx.CurrentBar() > (int)maSlopeLookback;
